WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.836
When you picture New York City, there are&nbsp;
so many iconic things that come to mind.

00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:08.869
But, before the yellow cabs
and hot dog&nbsp;stands,

00:00:08.869 --> 00:00:11.820
New York was known
for something else:

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.161
Oysters.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.400
From the 1600s through the 1800s,
New York was booming with them.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.874
And it was oysters, not hotdogs,
sold&nbsp;streetside by the millions.

00:00:22.874 --> 00:00:28.080
Oyster reefs covered over 220,000
acres along the coastline.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.329
The reefs were so large that ships&nbsp;
needed to navigate around them.

00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:36.174
But, of course,
this isn’t the case today.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.208
Oysters were overharvested
nearly out of&nbsp;existence,

00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:42.960
and not just in New York.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.230
Experts estimate we’ve lost 85% of the
world’s oyster reefs in the last 200 years.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.920
Today, we’re trying
to put them back.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.360
Because this animal that you&nbsp;
often find on a dinner plate

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.184
might actually be an effective&nbsp;
defense against the rising ocean.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.745
We’re losing our coasts
to climate change.

00:01:05.745 --> 00:01:08.720
As&nbsp;oceans levels rise,
the water erodes the shoreline.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.013
This pushes the
entire coast back,

00:01:11.013 --> 00:01:14.000
encroaching on homes
and destabilizing land.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.059
So, enter the oyster.

00:01:17.059 --> 00:01:18.960
This&nbsp;uncharismatic
rock of an animal.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.040
STEPHANIE WESTBY: Oh come on!
You&nbsp;don't think they're charismatic?

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.160
KIM: I feel like… It’s not&nbsp;
something I would call “cute.”

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.759
WESTBY: No, I can't
argue with you there.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:29.360
I've tried, but yeah,
no, they're not.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.093
Stephanie Westby has been
helping to&nbsp;restore oyster reefs

00:01:32.093 --> 00:01:34.867
in the US’s&nbsp;Chesapeake
Bay for over 10 years.

00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:38.400
WESTBY: Their charisma really lies in
their functionality, rather than their form.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.040
Oysters obviously
don't move around.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.360
And that’s exactly
part of the appeal.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.920
Oysters stick together.
Literally.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.379
Baby oysters called “spat” attach
to older&nbsp;and even dead oysters

00:01:49.379 --> 00:01:50.640
in order to grow.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.405
WESTBY: And over generations,
all of these oysters&nbsp;reproducing,

00:01:53.405 --> 00:01:54.960
it builds up the oyster reef.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.138
In some places, that sturdy reef
can help&nbsp;defend the coast

00:01:58.138 --> 00:02:00.773
by dampening&nbsp;
the force of incoming waves.

00:02:00.773 --> 00:02:04.492
WESTBY: If you have an
oyster reef that's&nbsp;"intertidal" --

00:02:04.492 --> 00:02:06.841
that sticks up at low tide --

00:02:06.841 --> 00:02:12.320
then it can perform some of that&nbsp;
wave energy protection function.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.080
Oyster reefs can break up waves&nbsp;
by catching the brunt of the force.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.620
Part of the wave is
deflected back to the ocean,

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.496
and the rest can more
gently reach the shoreline,

00:02:21.496 --> 00:02:23.836
which slows long-term erosion.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.560
On its own, an oyster reef won’t&nbsp;
*stop* a hurricane-level storm surge,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.195
but it could definitely
limit the damage.

00:02:31.195 --> 00:02:34.720
And the larger they grow, the&nbsp;
more protection they can offer:

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.404
As time goes on,
sea levels will rise.

00:02:37.404 --> 00:02:41.200
Unlike man-made breakwaters,
that will need to be rebuilt over time,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.489
oyster reefs just keep
growing upward.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.320
Various organizations around the world&nbsp;
are working to restore oyster reefs.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.960
But reef restoration isn’t as&nbsp;simple
as just dumping oysters into a bay.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.760
They need something to
stick to in order to grow.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.339
In New York, one organization
puts recycled&nbsp;shells in cages

00:03:03.339 --> 00:03:05.186
for oyster spat to grow on,

00:03:05.186 --> 00:03:07.554
and groups in Bangladesh,
and around the US,

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:12.734
have placed large concrete barriers
offshore for oyster spat to grow on.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:18.328
Now, on their own, concrete structures
like this&nbsp;are actually effective breakwaters.

00:03:18.328 --> 00:03:20.899
So... why add oysters?

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.404
To understand, it helps to look&nbsp;
at a more familiar type of reef:

00:03:26.404 --> 00:03:30.320
SEAN CORSON: Oyster reefs provide
much&nbsp;the same function as coral reefs.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.029
They provide the same kind of&nbsp;habitat.

00:03:33.029 --> 00:03:37.440
They are the underpinning of the&nbsp;
ecological systems where they exist,

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.518
just like coral reefs.

00:03:39.518 --> 00:03:41.920
Oysters are filtration systems.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.120
They eat by pulling in
large quantities of water.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.840
Algae, nitrogen, and other
contaminants are eaten,

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.072
or harmlessly dumped to the bottom
of&nbsp;the bay, and clean water is expelled.

00:03:52.072 --> 00:03:57.135
A single oyster can filter up to&nbsp;
50 gallons of water every day.

00:03:57.135 --> 00:04:01.680
As the water clarity improves, sea&nbsp;
grasses start to grow, fish return,

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.938
and other sea creatures make the&nbsp;
crevices in the reef their home.

00:04:04.938 --> 00:04:09.606
CORSON: They are this aggregating,&nbsp;
reef-building, hard structure.

00:04:09.606 --> 00:04:12.308
And so, if you look at
the way we try to&nbsp;deal with

00:04:12.308 --> 00:04:15.520
reducing erosion
right now, as a society,

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.698
for the most part, we put rocks,
big pieces&nbsp;of concrete,

00:04:19.698 --> 00:04:24.534
we set up, more or less, walls,
to try to slow the rate of waves,

00:04:24.534 --> 00:04:27.879
reduce the&nbsp;wind-driven
erosion, that type of thing.

00:04:27.879 --> 00:04:34.381
Oysters can serve in that capacity&nbsp;in
many ways, but bring added advantages.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.494
Places like New York City
or&nbsp;even the Chesapeake Bay

00:04:38.494 --> 00:04:42.997
are way too industrialized to
bring back the reefs of the&nbsp;1600s.

00:04:42.997 --> 00:04:45.840
But that’s not really the point.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.667
CORSON: I don't think we can
put it back just the way it was.

00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:53.280
I don't think that's
necessarily a realistic goal.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.002
But I think we've got a great opportunity&nbsp;
when we start thinking about

00:04:57.002 --> 00:05:01.103
multiple benefits, and the
different kinds of needs of society,

00:05:01.103 --> 00:05:05.840
whether it's to reduce wave impacts,
or offset nutrient inputs,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.858
or generally increasing the
health and resilience of the bay.

00:05:09.858 --> 00:05:12.945
Resiliency against the
rising oceans isn't&nbsp;as simple

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:15.418
as undoing the mistakes
we made in the past.

00:05:15.418 --> 00:05:20.080
We don’t live the way we did 200&nbsp;years ago,
and the world looks very different.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.373
But what we can learn from oysters, is&nbsp;
that restoring one species from the&nbsp;past

00:05:24.373 --> 00:05:28.221
can create a chain reaction&nbsp;
to a more sustainable future.

00:05:28.221 --> 00:05:30.689
CORSON: It feels hopeful.

00:05:30.689 --> 00:05:34.829
And&nbsp;it feels like something
that we can achieve.

